{
  "_comment": "The crest table: 22 named crests with the Dagdan Diadem names, the bearers confirmed in this game, the trigger rate and the Relic each one maps to.",
  "_mechanics": {
    "naming": "In Dagdan culture crests are called Diadems, and also Bloodmarks or Godmarks. This site keeps the series term crest alongside the in-world names so both searches land.",
    "scope": "Twenty-two named crests exist across the series. Twelve of them have a bearer confirmed in this game, and the rest are recorded here as crests with no bearer in this title rather than being left out.",
    "trigger": "Every recorded crest effect is a chance on attack, expressed as a percentage, so a crest is a reliability curve rather than a flat stat.",
    "relics": "The Hero~s Relic tied to each crest is known, and the effect of every relic is not yet documented by any source checked.",
    "stigma": "A Diadem granted by an unacknowledged god is read by some Dagdans as an ill omen, and its bearer can be shunned. Esmeralda carries Blaiddyd, which is the recorded case.",
    "conflict": "Ernest has a live source conflict: one source attributes its active effect to Anna while a wiki lineage page places it on Raksha. Both readings are recorded rather than one being chosen."
  },
  "_cursedObjects": {
    "summary": "Some bearers do not need to wield a cursed object at all, because it is embedded in their body.",
    "carriers": [
      "Cai and Theodora carry a cursed object embedded in their bodies, so they run Duality, the Gautier Diadem, natively with any weapon rather than only when holding the cursed weapon.",
      "Talimun~s Blaze Gauge behaviour suggests the same innate Duality.",
      "Orchel is recorded as a Duality bearer with no known cursed object, which is the case the embedded-object explanation does not cover.",
      "Cai carries two crests, Aubin and Gautier, side by side rather than one nested inside the other. His mother Aurora is recorded as an Aubin bearer."
    ],
    "naming": "Aurora is also the name of one of the six blessings sold at the Shrine of Causality. No source states whether the blessing and the character share a source, so the two are kept apart here rather than conflated."
  },
  "_blazeArts": {
    "what": "Blaze Arts are the heavy super-move class of action, and they are paid for in HP rather than in a resource bar.",
    "gauge": "Every use fills a gauge, and once that gauge fills the unit~s maximum HP is reduced.",
    "consequence": "That makes them a limited, high-risk resource rather than a rotation. The design intent is visible in the cost: you are trading a permanent slice of the unit~s durability for a burst of damage.",
    "gap": "No source checked publishes the move list, the individual effects, or the shrine path for strengthening them."
  },
  "_combatArts": {
    "what": "Combat Arts are special attacks that consume weapon durability when they are used.",
    "inheritance": "A Combat Art can be carried across when a Part 1 version of a unit is merged into a Part 3 unit through Merge Causality, which is how multi-route training compounds.",
    "gap": "No source checked publishes a Combat Art list or its learning conditions."
  },
  "_openGaps": [
    "No acquisition routes: no source records where any Diadem is obtained, which unit joins holding it, or in which chapter.",
    "No relic effects: every Hero~s Relic is named and none has its effect documented.",
    "No ability list: no source checked publishes the game~s Abilities, only the crest-triggered effects above.",
    "No Blaze Art moves: the mechanic is described, the moves are not."
  ],
